turn statements: return (\n            not direct,\n            not pinned,\n            requested_o\nraise statements: (none)\ncontrol flow: if=1 loops=0 try=1 with=0 returns=1 yields=0 awaits=0\n\nDiff from the previous version to this version:\n```diff\n--- previous\n+++ current\n@@ -33,18 +33,7 @@\n         requested_order = self._user_requested.get(identifier, math.inf)\n \n-        # Requires-Python has only one candidate and the check is basically\n-        # free, so we always do it first to avoid needless work if it fails.\n-        requires_python = identifier == REQUIRES_PYTHON_IDENTIFIER\n-\n-        # Prefer the causes of backtracking on the assumption that the problem\n-        # resolving the dependency tree is related to the failures that caused\n-        # the backtracking\n-        backtrack_cause = self.is_backtrack_cause(identifier, backtrack_causes)\n-\n         return (\n-            not requires_python,\n             not direct,\n             not pinned,\n-            not backtrack_cause,\n             requested_order,\n             not unfree,\n```\n\nStatements:\nS1. `get_preference` takes several parameters: an identifier for the requirement, mappings of resolutions and candidates, information about the requirements, and backtrack causes.\nS2. `get_preference` returns a 'Preference' tuple that serves as a sort key.\nS3. `get_preference` is a method in the `PipProvider` class.\nS4. The function first checks if there is any information available for the given identifier. If not, it sets `has_information` to False and skips certain calculations.\nS5. The function uses helper methods like `get_candidate_lookup` and `is_backtrack_cause`.\nS6. If the depth cannot be determined (eg: due to no matching parents), it is considered infinite.\nS7. The lower the return value is, the more preferred this group of arguments is.\nS8. If equal, Pip calculates an approximate 'depth' and resolves requirements closer to the user-specified requirements first.\nS